            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>The Cherokee Purple isn’t winning any beauty contests. After all, Craig joked, “it looks like a bruised leg.” But what this ‘mater lacks in looks, it makes up for in taste.</p><p><br></p><p>“The flavor is perfectly balanced between tart and sweet with good intensity,” said Craig, who helped rescue the heirloom from obscurity in 1990. “It’s kind of the everything tomato.”</p><p><br></p><p>For a simple Southern-style breakfast, split a warm buttermilk biscuit and top it with a fried egg, a thick slice of Cherokee Purple, and a generous smear of jalapeño pepper jelly. Enjoy it barefoot on the front porch with meemaw.</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
