          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>If the Cherokee Purple is smoky, the Cherokee Chocolate is downright smoldering. A dusky mutation that appeared in Craig’s garden in 1995, this mahogany-hued tomato packs rich, earthy flavor with just the right balance of sweetness and acidity.</p><p><br></p><p>“It has all the beauty and flavor of Cherokee Purple,” Craig said, “but with this deep, almost brick-red color.”</p><p>For a taste of Tuscany by way of Tennessee, tuck fried eggplant rounds, fresh mozzarella, and generous slices of Cherokee Chocolate into a toasted ciabatta roll. Add sun-dried tomato pesto, a smear of black olive tapenade, and some arugula for bite.</p><p><br></p><p>It’s smoky, savory, and just complex enough to impress your foodie friends. (Bonus points if you serve it with merlot and a mountain view.)</p></div>
